{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01A3ZfcgccHbcBNeXXR28Nt1","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t[\n2\t  {\n3\t    \"name\": \"sft_d100_qc_only\",\n4\t    \"description\": \"100% dosage SFT using only QC-pass paraphrases (7547/16697 items) on Qwen2.5-0.5B. Isolates noisy vs clean contamination confound. Compare calibration metrics against exp-001 d100 (all paraphrases) to quantify effect of noisy contamination on IRT estimates.\\n\",\n5\t    \"results\": {\n6\t      \"train_loss\": 1.58,\n7\t      \"token_accuracy\": 0.67,\n8\t      \"training_time_min\": 55,\n9\t      \"total_steps\": 2800,\n10\t      \"mmlu_accuracy\": 0.258,\n11\t      \"gsm8k_accuracy\": 0.355,\n12\t      \"arc_challenge_accuracy\": 0.255,\n13\t      \"humaneval_pass1\": 0.073,\n14\t      \"mmlu_delta_vs_d100_all\": 0.025,\n15\t      \"arc_delta_vs_d100_all\": -0.039,\n16\t      \"irt_gamma_qc_only\": -0.95,\n17\t      \"irt_gamma_qc_only_ci\": [\n18\t        -2.1,\n19\t        0.2\n20\t      ],\n21\t      \"irt_gamma_all_paraphrases\": -1.21,\n22\t      \"gamma_difference_significant\": false,\n23\t      \"mae_qc_only\": 0.038,\n24\t      \"mae_all_paraphrases\": 0.035,\n25\t      \"mae_gap_pp\": 0.003,\n26\t      \"coverage_95_qc_only\": 0.35,\n27\t      \"coverage_95_all\": 0.33\n28\t    },\n29\t    \"tables\": [\n30\t      {\n31\t        \"name\": \"Table: QC-only vs All-paraphrases comparison (0.5B, d100)\",\n32\t        \"columns\": [\n33\t          \"metric\",\n34\t          \"qc_only\",\n35\t          \"all_paraphrases\",\n36\t          \"delta\",\n37\t          \"significant\"\n38\t        ],\n39\t        \"rows\": [\n40\t          [\n41\t            \"MMLU accuracy\",\n42\t            0.258,\n43\t            0.233,\n44\t            0.025,\n45\t            \"no (p=0.18)\"\n46\t          ],\n47\t          [\n48\t            \"ARC accuracy\",\n49\t            0.255,\n50\t            0.294,\n51\t            -0.039,\n52\t            \"no (p=0.22)\"\n53\t          ],\n54\t          [\n55\t            \"GSM8K accuracy\",\n56\t            0.355,\n57\t            0.365,\n58\t            -0.01,\n59\t            \"no\"\n60\t          ],\n61\t          [\n62\t            \"IRT γ (ARC)\",\n63\t            -0.95,\n64\t            -1.21,\n65\t            0.26,\n66\t            \"no (CI overlap)\"\n67\t          ],\n68\t          [\n69\t            \"Calibrated MAE\",\n70\t            0.038,\n71\t            0.035,\n72\t            0.003,\n73\t            \"no\"\n74\t          ],\n75\t          [\n76\t            \"Coverage 95%\",\n77\t            0.35,\n78\t            0.33,\n79\t            0.02,\n80\t            \"no\"\n81\t          ]\n82\t        ]\n83\t      }\n84\t    ],\n85\t    \"figures\": [],\n86\t    \"analysis\": \"Result supports the identifiability-first narrative: the binding constraint on calibration quality is N_models (structural domain-method mismatch), not exposure signal noise. QC filtering improves signal quality but doesn't materially change IRT outcomes at N=8. Paper uses this as supplementary evidence that the 'When NOT to calibrate' guidance is driven by N limitations, not measurement error in Stage 1.\\n\\n\\nIn the median tier, QC-only filtering produces modestly cleaner contamination signal (higher per-item semantic fidelity) but removes 55% of items, reducing total contamination volume. The net effect on 0.5B is near-neutral: the model's limited capacity means it cannot fully exploit either the noisier-but-larger or cleaner-but-smaller contamination set. IRT γ estimate shifts slightly toward zero (less extreme forgetting) because QC-pass items have higher semantic similarity → less distribution shift → less catastrophic forgetting. However, the difference is NOT statistically significant (overlapping CIs) because N=8 models provides insufficient power to distinguish the two conditions. This supports the paper's narrative that contamination quality modulates effect size but the IRT framework's primary limitation is N, not signal noise. Calibration MAE remains comparable — the noise propagation finding (exp-005: CI insensitive to σ_e) already showed that exposure noise is not the binding constraint.\\n\"\n87\t  },\n88\t  {\n89\t    \"name\": \"scale_bridging_7b\",\n90\t    \"description\": \"Qwen2.5-7B controlled SFT injection at 5 dosage levels (0%, 5%, 25%, 50%, 100%). Validates calibration accuracy scales to larger models. Multi-GPU node required.\\n\",\n91\t    \"results\": {\n92\t      \"training_hours_per_run\": 6.5,\n93\t      \"total_gpu_hours\": 32.5,\n94\t      \"d000_mmlu\": 0.715,\n95\t      \"d000_gsm8k\": 0.785,\n96\t      \"d000_arc\": 0.885,\n97\t      \"d000_humaneval\": 0.28,\n98\t      \"d005_mmlu\": 0.716,\n99\t      \"d005_gsm8k\": 0.787,\n100\t      \"d005_arc\": 0.884,\n101\t      \"d005_humaneval\": 0.295,\n102\t      \"d025_mmlu\": 0.718,\n103\t      \"d025_gsm8k\": 0.79,\n104\t      \"d025_arc\": 0.883,\n105\t      \"d025_humaneval\": 0.31,\n106\t      \"d100_mmlu\": 0.722,\n107\t      \"d100_gsm8k\": 0.795,\n108\t      \"d100_arc\": 0.878,\n109\t      \"d100_humaneval\": 0.35,\n110\t      \"mmlu_max_delta_pp\": 0.7,\n111\t      \"gsm8k_max_delta_pp\": 1.0,\n112\t      \"arc_max_delta_pp\": -0.7,\n113\t      \"humaneval_max_delta_pp\": 7.0,\n114\t      \"arc_gamma_7b_only\": -0.15,\n115\t      \"arc_gamma_7b_ci\": [\n116\t        -0.8,\n117\t        0.5\n118\t      ],\n119\t      \"arc_gamma_ci_excludes_zero\": false,\n120\t      \"mmlu_gamma_7b_only\": 0.08,\n121\t      \"mmlu_gamma_7b_ci\": [\n122\t        -0.6,\n123\t        0.7\n124\t      ],\n125\t      \"s1_d005_auc_7b\": 0.96,\n126\t      \"s2_d005_auc_7b\": 0.52,\n127\t      \"s2_d005_cohens_d_7b\": 0.08,\n128\t      \"mae_calibrated_7b\": 0.004,\n129\t      \"mae_raw_7b\": 0.003,\n130\t      \"calibration_unnecessary\": true\n131\t    },\n132\t    \"tables\": [\n133\t      {\n134\t        \"name\": \"Table: Contamination effect by scale (ARC-Challenge)\",\n135\t        \"columns\": [\n136\t          \"scale\",\n137\t          \"d000_acc\",\n138\t          \"d100_acc\",\n139\t          \"delta_pp\",\n140\t          \"gamma\",\n141\t          \"gamma_ci_excludes_zero\"\n142\t        ],\n143\t        \"rows\": [\n144\t          [\n145\t            \"0.5B\",\n146\t            0.47,\n147\t            0.294,\n148\t            -17.6,\n149\t            -1.21,\n150\t            \"borderline (0.12)\"\n151\t          ],\n152\t          [\n153\t            \"1.5B\",\n154\t            0.772,\n155\t            0.769,\n156\t            -0.3,\n157\t            -0.18,\n158\t            \"no\"\n159\t          ],\n160\t          [\n161\t            \"3B\",\n162\t            0.828,\n163\t            0.84,\n164\t            1.2,\n165\t            -0.42,\n166\t            \"no\"\n167\t          ],\n168\t          [\n169\t            \"7B\",\n170\t            0.885,\n171\t            0.878,\n172\t            -0.7,\n173\t            -0.15,\n174\t            \"no\"\n175\t          ]\n176\t        ]\n177\t      },\n178\t      {\n179\t        \"name\": \"Table: Three-regime taxonomy evidence\",\n180\t        \"columns\": [\n181\t          \"regime\",\n182\t          \"scale\",\n183\t          \"arc_delta_pp\",\n184\t          \"mechanism\",\n185\t          \"gamma_significant\"\n186\t        ],\n187\t        \"rows\": [\n188\t          [\n189\t            \"Catastrophic forgetting\",\n190\t            \"0.5B\",\n191\t            -17.6,\n192\t            \"Capacity displacement\",\n193\t            \"borderline\"\n194\t          ],\n195\t          [\n196\t            \"Mild perturbation\",\n197\t            \"1.5B-3B\",\n198\t            \"-0.3 to +1.2\",\n199\t            \"Absorbed without displacement\",\n200\t            \"no\"\n201\t          ],\n202\t          [\n203\t            \"Near-immunity\",\n204\t            \"7B\",\n205\t            -0.7,\n206\t            \"Negligible relative to capacity\",\n207\t            \"no\"\n208\t          ]\n209\t        ]\n210\t      }\n211\t    ],\n212\t    \"figures\": [],\n213\t    \"analysis\": \"7B data strengthens the scale-dependent characterization but weakens the overall statistical case for γ as a useful calibration parameter. At 7B, γ≈0 means ContamCal correctly identifies 'no calibration needed' — this is actually a success of the gating criterion, not a failure. Paper narrative: ContamCal's value at large scales is diagnostic (confirming no contamination effect) rather than corrective. The three-regime taxonomy holds directionally but only the 0.5B regime has borderline-significant γ when fitted alone — the pooled N=24 analysis is needed for statistical power.\\n\\n\\nIn the median tier, 7B demonstrates near-immunity to MC benchmark contamination via SFT, confirming the scale-dependent characterization. The mechanism: 7B has sufficient capacity that SFT contamination occupies a negligible fraction of model parameters — no catastrophic forgetting (unlike 0.5B) and no detectable inflation on MC benchmarks (unlike the pattern at smaller scales). ARC shows a tiny negative trend (-0.7pp at d100) but this is within noise. HumanEval shows the only meaningful dose-response (+7pp), consistent with 1.5B findings where code generation was the only benchmark affected at larger scales.\\nIRT γ at 7B alone is effectively zero with wide CI — contamination effect is undetectable at this scale. This is the \\\"near-immunity\\\" regime in the three-regime taxonomy. The critical implication: calibration is unnecessary at 7B (raw MAE already < 0.5pp), and IRT correctly identifies this via non-significant γ. This validates the calibration gating criterion: when γ CI includes zero, report raw scores.\\nS2 (Min-K++) at 7B shows d≈0 (neither reversed nor effective) — distinct from the directional reversal at 0.5B. This narrows the \\\"signal failure\\\" claim to be explicitly scale-dependent: reversal at 0.5B, ineffectiveness at 3B/7B, unknown at larger scales. The reduced statistical strength in median tier means we cannot claim a clean monotonic relationship between scale and γ magnitude — just the directional pattern.\\n\"\n214\t  },\n215\t  {\n216\t    \"name\": \"multi_family_validation\",\n217\t    \"description\": \"Controlled SFT injection on Llama-3.2-1B at 3 dosage levels (0%, 25%, 100%). Validates that identifiability boundaries and calibration accuracy generalize beyond Qwen2.5. Addresses single model family limitation.\\n\",\n218\t    \"results\": {\n219\t      \"training_hours_per_run\": 3.5,\n220\t      \"total_gpu_hours\": 10.5,\n221\t      \"d000_mmlu\": 0.465,\n222\t      \"d000_gsm8k\": 0.42,\n223\t      \"d000_arc\": 0.625,\n224\t      \"d000_humaneval\": 0.095,\n225\t      \"d025_mmlu\": 0.46,\n226\t      \"d025_gsm8k\": 0.425,\n227\t      \"d025_arc\": 0.61,\n228\t      \"d025_humaneval\": 0.11,\n229\t      \"d100_mmlu\": 0.445,\n230\t      \"d100_gsm8k\": 0.435,\n231\t      \"d100_arc\": 0.57,\n232\t      \"d100_humaneval\": 0.125,\n233\t      \"arc_delta_d100_pp\": -5.5,\n234\t      \"mmlu_delta_d100_pp\": -2.0,\n235\t      \"gsm8k_delta_d100_pp\": 1.5,\n236\t      \"llama_vs_qwen_susceptibility_ratio\": 0.31,\n237\t      \"arc_gamma_llama\": -0.75,\n238\t      \"arc_gamma_llama_ci\": [\n239\t        -1.8,\n240\t        0.3\n241\t      ],\n242\t      \"arc_gamma_ci_excludes_zero\": false,\n243\t      \"gamma_sign_consistent_arc\": true,\n244\t      \"gamma_sign_consistent_gsm8k\": true,\n245\t      \"gamma_magnitude_ratio_llama_vs_qwen\": 0.55,\n246\t      \"multi_family_gamma_rank_tau\": 0.6,\n247\t      \"s1_d005_auc_llama\": 0.94,\n248\t      \"s2_d005_auc_llama\": 0.48,\n249\t      \"s2_d100_cohens_d_llama\": -0.85\n250\t    },\n251\t    \"tables\": [\n252\t      {\n253\t        \"name\": \"Table: Cross-family contamination susceptibility\",\n254\t        \"columns\": [\n255\t          \"family\",\n256\t          \"scale\",\n257\t          \"arc_d100_delta_pp\",\n258\t          \"arc_gamma\",\n259\t          \"gamma_sign\",\n260\t          \"ci_excludes_zero\"\n261\t        ],\n262\t        \"rows\": [\n263\t          [\n264\t            \"Qwen2.5\",\n265\t            \"0.5B\",\n266\t            -17.6,\n267\t            -1.21,\n268\t            \"negative\",\n269\t            \"borderline\"\n270\t          ],\n271\t          [\n272\t            \"Qwen2.5\",\n273\t            \"1.5B\",\n274\t            -0.3,\n275\t            -0.18,\n276\t            \"negative\",\n277\t            \"no\"\n278\t          ],\n279\t          [\n280\t            \"Qwen2.5\",\n281\t            \"3B\",\n282\t            1.2,\n283\t            -0.42,\n284\t            \"negative\",\n285\t            \"no\"\n286\t          ],\n287\t          [\n288\t            \"Llama-3.2\",\n289\t            \"1B\",\n290\t            -5.5,\n291\t            -0.75,\n292\t            \"negative\",\n293\t            \"no\"\n294\t          ]\n295\t        ]\n296\t      },\n297\t      {\n298\t        \"name\": \"Table: Signal effectiveness cross-family\",\n299\t        \"columns\": [\n300\t          \"signal\",\n301\t          \"metric\",\n302\t          \"Qwen_0.5B\",\n303\t          \"Llama_1B\",\n304\t          \"consistent\"\n305\t        ],\n306\t        \"rows\": [\n307\t          [\n308\t            \"S1 embedding\",\n309\t            \"d005 AUC\",\n310\t            0.979,\n311\t            0.94,\n312\t            \"yes\"\n313\t          ],\n314\t          [\n315\t            \"S2 Min-K++\",\n316\t            \"d005 AUC\",\n317\t            0.465,\n318\t            0.48,\n319\t            \"yes (both fail)\"\n320\t          ],\n321\t          [\n322\t            \"S2 Min-K++\",\n323\t            \"d100 Cohen's d\",\n324\t            -1.98,\n325\t            -0.85,\n326\t            \"yes (both negative)\"\n327\t          ],\n328\t          [\n329\t            \"S4 Self-Critique\",\n330\t            \"d005 AUC\",\n331\t            0.481,\n332\t            0.5,\n333\t            \"yes (both fail)\"\n334\t          ]\n335\t        ]\n336\t      }\n337\t    ],\n338\t    \"figures\": [],\n339\t    \"analysis\": \"Multi-family result confirms contamination susceptibility is partially a benchmark property (ARC consistently shows forgetting) but magnitude is model-family-dependent. This weakens the strongest form of the characterization claim ('contamination is a benchmark property') to the softer 'contamination direction is benchmark-determined but magnitude is family-modulated.' Kendall τ=0.60 provides moderate support. The paper presents this honestly: 'directional consistency across families with heterogeneous magnitude suggests contamination characterization requires per-family calibration for quantitative use.'\\n\\n\\nIn the median tier, Llama-3.2-1B shows the same directional pattern as Qwen (ARC forgetting, GSM8K mild inflation) but with substantially reduced magnitude. The γ sign is consistent (negative for ARC) but magnitude is roughly half that of Qwen at comparable scale. This is expected: Llama-3.2-1B has different architecture (GQA, different tokenizer, different pre-training data distribution) which affects how SFT contamination interacts with stored knowledge. The rank correlation (τ=0.60) is moderate — consistent direction but different magnitude means the benchmarks don't perfectly rank-order in susceptibility across families.\\nCritically, the γ CI for Llama alone does NOT exclude zero — this is the reduced statistical strength characteristic of median tier. Only when pooled with Qwen data (raising N toward 24) does the combined γ become borderline significant. This means the multi-family finding is 'directionally consistent' but not independently confirmatory at the individual-family level.\\nS2 (Min-K++) shows a weaker reversal on Llama (d=-0.85 vs d=-1.98 on Qwen at 0.5B), still negative but less extreme. This is consistent with the 'scale-dependent' signal failure narrative: Llama-3.2-1B at 1B parameters is between 0.5B and 1.5B Qwen in terms of effective capacity, so the reversal is attenuated. S1 remains robust (AUC=0.94).\\n\"\n340\t  },\n341\t  {\n342\t    \"name\": \"rl_observational_case_study\",\n343\t    \"description\": \"Apply Self-Critique + available signals to existing RL-trained models: Qwen2.5-Instruct (0.5B/1.5B/3B/7B), DeepSeek-R1-Distill-1.5B/7B. Fit IRT with wider CIs. Expected regret quantification for model selection. Near-zero γ check on known-clean benchmark (specificity test).\\n\",\n344\t    \"results\": {\n345\t      \"n_models_evaluated\": 8,\n346\t      \"s1_computable\": true,\n347\t      \"s2_computable\": true,\n348\t      \"s4_computable\": true,\n349\t      \"s1_auc_instruct_d005_equivalent\": 0.72,\n350\t      \"s2_instruct_mean_score\": 1.69,\n351\t      \"s4_instruct_mean_score\": -0.91,\n352\t      \"arc_gamma_observational\": -0.52,\n353\t      \"arc_gamma_observational_ci\": [\n354\t        -1.8,\n355\t        0.8\n356\t      ],\n357\t      \"arc_gamma_ci_excludes_zero\": false,\n358\t      \"mmlu_gamma_observational\": 0.18,\n359\t      \"mmlu_gamma_observational_ci\": [\n360\t        -0.9,\n361\t        1.3\n362\t      ],\n363\t      \"gsm8k_gamma_observational\": 0.35,\n364\t      \"gsm8k_gamma_observational_ci\": [\n365\t        -0.6,\n366\t        1.3\n367\t      ],\n368\t      \"specificity_gamma\": 0.05,\n369\t      \"specificity_gamma_ci\": [\n370\t        -0.7,\n371\t        0.8\n372\t      ],\n373\t      \"specificity_passes\": true,\n374\t      \"mean_ci_width_observational\": 0.12,\n375\t      \"mean_ci_width_controlled\": 0.07,\n376\t      \"ci_ratio\": 1.7,\n377\t      \"regret_raw_top5\": 0.015,\n378\t      \"regret_calibrated_top5\": 0.018,\n379\t      \"regret_remove_rescore_top5\": 0.012,\n380\t      \"calibration_improves_selection\": false,\n381\t      \"deepseek_r1_arc_gamma_contribution\": -0.3,\n382\t      \"deepseek_s1_score_elevated\": true\n383\t    },\n384\t    \"tables\": [\n385\t      {\n386\t        \"name\": \"Table: Observational γ estimates (Instruct + DeepSeek models)\",\n387\t        \"columns\": [\n388\t          \"benchmark\",\n389\t          \"gamma\",\n390\t          \"ci_lower\",\n391\t          \"ci_upper\",\n392\t          \"ci_excludes_zero\",\n393\t          \"interpretation\"\n394\t        ],\n395\t        \"rows\": [\n396\t          [\n397\t            \"ARC-Challenge\",\n398\t            -0.52,\n399\t            -1.8,\n400\t            0.8,\n401\t            \"no\",\n402\t            \"Directionally consistent with controlled (forgetting), underpowered\"\n403\t          ],\n404\t          [\n405\t            \"MMLU\",\n406\t            0.18,\n407\t            -0.9,\n408\t            1.3,\n409\t            \"no\",\n410\t            \"Near-zero, consistent with scale-dependent attenuation\"\n411\t          ],\n412\t          [\n413\t            \"GSM8K\",\n414\t            0.35,\n415\t            -0.6,\n416\t            1.3,\n417\t            \"no\",\n418\t            \"Non-significant, consistent with gating rejection\"\n419\t          ],\n420\t          [\n421\t            \"TriviaQA (specificity)\",\n422\t            0.05,\n423\t            -0.7,\n424\t            0.8,\n425\t            \"no\",\n426\t            \"PASS — no false positive\"\n427\t          ]\n428\t        ]\n429\t      },\n430\t      {\n431\t        \"name\": \"Table: Expected regret — top-5 model selection\",\n432\t        \"columns\": [\n433\t          \"method\",\n434\t          \"regret_pp\",\n435\t          \"rank_tau_vs_clean\",\n436\t          \"ci_width\"\n437\t        ],\n438\t        \"rows\": [\n439\t          [\n440\t            \"Raw scores\",\n441\t            1.5,\n442\t            0.85,\n443\t            \"N/A\"\n444\t          ],\n445\t          [\n446\t            \"ContamCal calibrated\",\n447\t            1.8,\n448\t            0.8,\n449\t            0.12\n450\t          ],\n451\t          [\n452\t            \"Remove-and-rescore\",\n453\t            1.2,\n454\t            0.88,\n455\t            \"N/A\"\n456\t          ],\n457\t          [\n458\t            \"Linear regression\",\n459\t            1.6,\n460\t            0.83,\n461\t            0.08\n462\t          ]\n463\t        ]\n464\t      }\n465\t    ],\n466\t    \"figures\": [],\n467\t    \"analysis\": \"The observational case study is an honest demonstration of ContamCal's limitations in the wild. Without controlled dosage variation, the framework correctly identifies directional effects but lacks power for quantitative calibration. This supports the paper's 'When NOT to calibrate' section: observational settings with large models (≥1.5B) and no dosage variation are below the identifiability threshold. The specificity check is the key positive finding — validating that the framework doesn't produce false contamination diagnoses. Paper frames this as 'ContamCal as diagnostic tool (is there contamination?) rather than corrective tool (what's the true score?) in observational settings.'\\n\\n\\nIn the median tier, the observational case study produces directional but not statistically significant findings. Without ground-truth contamination labels, the IRT fit relies entirely on Stage 1 signal scores as exposure proxies. S1 (embedding similarity) on Instruct models is noisier than on controlled SFT models because RL post-training alters the relationship between training data exposure and embedding space proximity.\\nARC γ is negative (consistent with controlled findings) but CI widely includes zero — the observational setting lacks the statistical power of the controlled experiment. This is expected: without dosage variation, all models cluster near similar exposure levels, collapsing the range that IRT needs to estimate γ reliably.\\nExpected regret analysis shows calibration does NOT improve model selection in this setting. Raw scores already rank models reasonably well (because contamination effects at 1.5B+ are small per the scale-dependent finding). Remove-and-rescore performs marginally better because it simply drops high-exposure items without the risk of IRT miscalibration. This is an honest negative result that reinforces the 'When NOT to calibrate' message: in observational settings with no dosage variation and large models, calibration overhead is not justified.\\nSpecificity check passes: γ≈0 on TriviaQA (a benchmark not in training data) confirms the framework doesn't hallucinate contamination effects where none exist. This is a valuable positive control.\\n\"\n468\t  },\n469\t  {\n470\t    \"name\": \"observational_72b\",\n471\t    \"description\": \"Qwen2.5-72B observational analysis (inference only, no SFT). Extends ecological validity to frontier model scale.\\n\",\n472\t    \"results\": {\n473\t      \"inference_gpu_hours\": 12,\n474\t      \"mmlu_accuracy\": 0.842,\n475\t      \"gsm8k_accuracy\": 0.905,\n476\t      \"arc_challenge_accuracy\": 0.945,\n477\t      \"humaneval_pass1\": 0.685,\n478\t      \"s1_mean_exposure_mmlu\": 0.42,\n479\t      \"s1_mean_exposure_arc\": 0.38,\n480\t      \"s1_mean_exposure_gsm8k\": 0.35,\n481\t      \"s2_minkpp_mean\": 1.72,\n482\t      \"s4_selfcritique_mean\": -0.88,\n483\t      \"contribution_to_pooled_n\": 1,\n484\t      \"pooled_n_after_72b\": 9,\n485\t      \"s1_72b_vs_3b_delta\": 0.05,\n486\t      \"s1_72b_relative_rank\": \"middle (not highest, not lowest)\",\n487\t      \"position_bias_72b_mmlu\": 0.02,\n488\t      \"position_bias_72b_arc\": 0.015,\n489\t      \"position_bias_absent\": true\n490\t    },\n491\t    \"tables\": [\n492\t      {\n493\t        \"name\": \"Table: 72B observational results\",\n494\t        \"columns\": [\n495\t          \"benchmark\",\n496\t          \"accuracy\",\n497\t          \"s1_mean_exposure\",\n498\t          \"interpretation\"\n499\t        ],\n500\t        \"rows\": [\n501\t          [\n502\t            \"MMLU\",\n503\t            0.842,\n504\t            0.42,\n505\t            \"Strong performance, moderate background exposure\"\n506\t          ],\n507\t          [\n508\t            \"ARC-Challenge\",\n509\t            0.945,\n510\t            0.38,\n511\t            \"Near-ceiling, exposure ≠ inflation\"\n512\t          ],\n513\t          [\n514\t            \"GSM8K\",\n515\t            0.905,\n516\t            0.35,\n517\t            \"Strong, below ARC/MMLU exposure\"\n518\t          ],\n519\t          [\n520\t            \"HumanEval\",\n521\t            0.685,\n522\t            0.28,\n523\t            \"Lowest exposure (code is harder to memorize)\"\n524\t          ]\n525\t        ]\n526\t      }\n527\t    ],\n528\t    \"figures\": [],\n529\t    \"analysis\": \"72B is appendix-level evidence. Its primary value is completeness (showing the framework applies at frontier scale) and the position bias absence confirmation. Does not change any statistical conclusions. In median tier, this experiment is 'included for ecological validity' — one sentence in the main text, details in appendix.\\n\\n\\nIn the median tier, 72B provides ecological validity but minimal statistical contribution. A single model cannot independently estimate γ — it only adds one data point to the pooled analysis. The 72B model's near-ceiling performance on ARC (94.5%) and strong performance on MMLU (84.2%) means contamination effects, if any, are masked by the model's genuine capability. This is the 'near-immunity' regime taken to its extreme.\\nS1 exposure scores are moderate (not zero, because 72B's training data likely includes benchmark-adjacent content given its massive training corpus), but this doesn't imply contamination harm — it's the expected background exposure level for a frontier model. The key contribution is confirming that position bias (which invalidated 0.5B MC data) is absent at 72B, validating that logprob-based MC evaluation is reliable at frontier scale.\\nThe finding supports the scale-dependent narrative without adding statistical power. Paper uses 72B as a single sentence: 'Contamination effect at frontier scale (72B) is undetectable, consistent with the three-regime taxonomy.'\\n\"\n530\t  },\n531\t  {\n532\t    \"name\": \"closed_weight_2signal_mode\",\n533\t    \"description\": \"Degraded 2-signal mode (ConStat + Self-Critique, no logits/embeddings) for closed-weight models. Ablation quantifying 2-signal vs 4-signal calibration accuracy gap.\\n\",\n534\t    \"results\": {\n535\t      \"n_signals\": 2,\n536\t      \"s3_constat_d005_auc\": 0.55,\n537\t      \"s4_selfcritique_d005_auc\": 0.48,\n538\t      \"rrf_2signal_d005_auc\": 0.56,\n539\t      \"rrf_4signal_d005_auc\": 0.97,\n540\t      \"auc_degradation\": 0.41,\n541\t      \"arc_gamma_2signal\": -0.35,\n542\t      \"arc_gamma_2signal_ci\": [\n543\t        -2.5,\n544\t        1.8\n545\t      ],\n546\t      \"arc_gamma_ci_excludes_zero\": false,\n547\t      \"mae_2signal\": 0.065,\n548\t      \"mae_4signal\": 0.035,\n549\t      \"mae_raw\": 0.059,\n550\t      \"coverage_95_2signal\": 0.25,\n551\t      \"coverage_95_4signal\": 0.35,\n552\t      \"calibration_improves_over_raw\": false,\n553\t      \"mae_gap_2signal_vs_raw\": 0.006,\n554\t      \"kendall_tau_2signal\": 0.55,\n555\t      \"kendall_tau_4signal\": 0.76\n556\t    },\n557\t    \"tables\": [\n558\t      {\n559\t        \"name\": \"Table: 2-signal vs 4-signal calibration comparison\",\n560\t        \"columns\": [\n561\t          \"metric\",\n562\t          \"2_signal\",\n563\t          \"4_signal\",\n564\t          \"raw\",\n565\t          \"winner\"\n566\t        ],\n567\t        \"rows\": [\n568\t          [\n569\t            \"Composite AUC\",\n570\t            0.56,\n571\t            0.97,\n572\t            \"N/A\",\n573\t            \"4-signal\"\n574\t          ],\n575\t          [\n576\t            \"γ CI width\",\n577\t            4.3,\n578\t            2.65,\n579\t            \"N/A\",\n580\t            \"4-signal\"\n581\t          ],\n582\t          [\n583\t            \"γ excludes zero\",\n584\t            \"no\",\n585\t            \"borderline\",\n586\t            \"N/A\",\n587\t            \"4-signal\"\n588\t          ],\n589\t          [\n590\t            \"MAE\",\n591\t            0.065,\n592\t            0.035,\n593\t            0.059,\n594\t            \"4-signal\"\n595\t          ],\n596\t          [\n597\t            \"Coverage 95%\",\n598\t            0.25,\n599\t            0.35,\n600\t            \"N/A\",\n601\t            \"4-signal\"\n602\t          ],\n603\t          [\n604\t            \"Kendall τ\",\n605\t            0.55,\n606\t            0.76,\n607\t            0.82,\n608\t            \"raw\"\n609\t          ]\n610\t        ]\n611\t      },\n612\t      {\n613\t        \"name\": \"Table: Signal AUC by mode\",\n614\t        \"columns\": [\n615\t          \"signal\",\n616\t          \"available_closed_weight\",\n617\t          \"d005_auc\",\n618\t          \"contribution\"\n619\t        ],\n620\t        \"rows\": [\n621\t          [\n622\t            \"S1 embedding\",\n623\t            \"no (requires weights)\",\n624\t            0.979,\n625\t            \"dominant\"\n626\t          ],\n627\t          [\n628\t            \"S2 Min-K++\",\n629\t            \"no (requires logits)\",\n630\t            0.465,\n631\t            \"none (chance)\"\n632\t          ],\n633\t          [\n634\t            \"S3 ConStat\",\n635\t            \"yes\",\n636\t            0.55,\n637\t            \"marginal\"\n638\t          ],\n639\t          [\n640\t            \"S4 Self-Critique\",\n641\t            \"yes\",\n642\t            0.48,\n643\t            \"none (chance)\"\n644\t          ]\n645\t        ]\n646\t      }\n647\t    ],\n648\t    \"figures\": [],\n649\t    \"analysis\": \"The 2-signal mode is a confirmed failure mode that the paper presents transparently. This strengthens the honest 'limitations' section and supports the broader narrative: contamination calibration fundamentally requires access to model internals (at minimum, embedding space) because surface-level signals (performance gaps, entropy patterns) lack discriminative power at the item level. In median tier, this negative result is not surprising — it follows directly from exp-008 showing S3/S4 at chance — but it's the formal ablation needed for the paper. Contributes to the 'When NOT to calibrate' section: 'Do not attempt calibration when (a) only closed-weight signals available, (b) composite signal AUC < 0.60.'\\n\\nIn the median tier, the 2-signal mode is essentially non-functional for calibration. Both ConStat and Self-Critique showed AUC≈0.5 (chance level) at 0.5B in exp-008. Without S1 (the only effective signal), the exposure scores fed to IRT are near-random, making γ estimation impossible. The resulting γ CI is extremely wide and includes zero — IRT cannot distinguish contaminated from clean items when the input signal is noise.\\nCalibration MAE is WORSE than raw (0.065 vs 0.059) because the noisy exposure scores introduce systematic error: IRT attempts to 'correct' based on meaningless signal variation, producing biased counterfactual estimates. This is the calibration gating criterion in action: when input signal AUC < 0.55 (per exp-004 simulation), calibration should not be attempted.\\nThis result is presented honestly as a hard limitation: ContamCal requires model weights (for S1 embedding similarity) to function. Closed-weight evaluation is restricted to ConStat's performance gap approach, which requires access to both original and paraphrased items but not model internals — however, this alone is insufficient for reliable calibration. Paper frames: 'closed-weight calibration remains an open problem; ContamCal's requirement for embedding access is a genuine limitation, not a design choice that could be easily relaxed.'\\n\"\n650\t  }\n651\t]"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"865006b4-9385-4d92-9192-c5ee14d98053","uuid":"1d766b45-9d60-4963-89c8-a9d0aa973a09","timestamp":"2026-05-16T11:15:54.618Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_13/projects/contamcal-irt-calibration/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260516T104822/median/experiment_data.json","content":"[\n  {\n    \"name\": \"sft_d100_qc_only\",\n    \"description\": \"100% dosage SFT using only QC-pass paraphrases (7547/16697 items) on Qwen2.5-0.5B. Isolates noisy vs clean contamination confound. Compare calibration metrics against exp-001 d100 (all paraphrases) to quantify effect of noisy contamination on IRT estimates.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"train_loss\": 1.58,\n      \"token_accuracy\": 0.67,\n      \"training_time_min\": 55,\n      \"total_steps\": 2800,\n      \"mmlu_accuracy\": 0.258,\n      \"gsm8k_accuracy\": 0.355,\n      \"arc_challenge_accuracy\": 0.255,\n      \"humaneval_pass1\": 0.073,\n      \"mmlu_delta_vs_d100_all\": 0.025,\n      \"arc_delta_vs_d100_all\": -0.039,\n      \"irt_gamma_qc_only\": -0.95,\n      \"irt_gamma_qc_only_ci\": [\n        -2.1,\n        0.2\n      ],\n      \"irt_gamma_all_paraphrases\": -1.21,\n      \"gamma_difference_significant\": false,\n      \"mae_qc_only\": 0.038,\n      \"mae_all_paraphrases\": 0.035,\n      \"mae_gap_pp\": 0.003,\n      \"coverage_95_qc_only\": 0.35,\n      \"coverage_95_all\": 0.33\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table: QC-only vs All-paraphrases comparison (0.5B, d100)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"metric\",\n          \"qc_only\",\n          \"all_paraphrases\",\n          \"delta\",\n          \"significant\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"MMLU accuracy\",\n            0.258,\n            0.233,\n            0.025,\n            \"no (p=0.18)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"ARC accuracy\",\n            0.255,\n            0.294,\n            -0.039,\n            \"no (p=0.22)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"GSM8K accuracy\",\n            0.355,\n            0.365,\n            -0.01,\n            \"no\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"IRT γ (ARC)\",\n            -0.95,\n            -1.21,\n            0.26,\n            \"no (CI overlap)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Calibrated MAE\",\n            0.038,\n            0.035,\n            0.003,\n            \"no\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Coverage 95%\",\n            0.35,\n            0.33,\n            0.02,\n            \"no\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Result supports the identifiability-first narrative: the binding constraint on calibration quality is N_models (structural domain-method mismatch), not exposure signal noise. QC filtering improves signal quality but doesn't materially change IRT outcomes at N=8. Paper uses this as supplementary evidence that the 'When NOT to calibrate' guidance is driven by N limitations, not measurement error in Stage 1.\\n\\n\\nIn the median tier, QC-only filtering produces modestly cleaner contamination signal (higher per-item semantic fidelity) but removes 55% of items, reducing total contamination volume. The net effect on 0.5B is near-neutral: the model's limited capacity means it cannot fully exploit either the noisier-but-larger or cleaner-but-smaller contamination set. IRT γ estimate shifts slightly toward zero (less extreme forgetting) because QC-pass items have higher semantic similarity → less distribution shift → less catastrophic forgetting. However, the difference is NOT statistically significant (overlapping CIs) because N=8 models provides insufficient power to distinguish the two conditions. This supports the paper's narrative that contamination quality modulates effect size but the IRT framework's primary limitation is N, not signal noise. Calibration MAE remains comparable — the noise propagation finding (exp-005: CI insensitive to σ_e) already showed that exposure noise is not the binding constraint.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"scale_bridging_7b\",\n    \"description\": \"Qwen2.5-7B controlled SFT injection at 5 dosage levels (0%, 5%, 25%, 50%, 100%). Validates calibration accuracy scales to larger models. Multi-GPU node required.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"training_hours_per_run\": 6.5,\n      \"total_gpu_hours\": 32.5,\n      \"d000_mmlu\": 0.715,\n      \"d000_gsm8k\": 0.785,\n      \"d000_arc\": 0.885,\n      \"d000_humaneval\": 0.28,\n      \"d005_mmlu\": 0.716,\n      \"d005_gsm8k\": 0.787,\n      \"d005_arc\": 0.884,\n      \"d005_humaneval\": 0.295,\n      \"d025_mmlu\": 0.718,\n      \"d025_gsm8k\": 0.79,\n      \"d025_arc\": 0.883,\n      \"d025_humaneval\": 0.31,\n      \"d100_mmlu\": 0.722,\n      \"d100_gsm8k\": 0.795,\n      \"d100_arc\": 0.878,\n      \"d100_humaneval\": 0.35,\n      \"mmlu_max_delta_pp\": 0.7,\n      \"gsm8k_max_delta_pp\": 1.0,\n      \"arc_max_delta_pp\": -0.7,\n      \"humaneval_max_delta_pp\": 7.0,\n      \"arc_gamma_7b_only\": -0.15,\n      \"arc_gamma_7b_ci\": [\n        -0.8,\n        0.5\n      ],\n      \"arc_gamma_ci_excludes_zero\": false,\n      \"mmlu_gamma_7b_only\": 0.08,\n      \"mmlu_gamma_7b_ci\": [\n        -0.6,\n        0.7\n      ],\n      \"s1_d005_auc_7b\": 0.96,\n      \"s2_d005_auc_7b\": 0.52,\n      \"s2_d005_cohens_d_7b\": 0.08,\n      \"mae_calibrated_7b\": 0.004,\n      \"mae_raw_7b\": 0.003,\n      \"calibration_unnecessary\": true\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table: Contamination effect by scale (ARC-Challenge)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"scale\",\n          \"d000_acc\",\n          \"d100_acc\",\n          \"delta_pp\",\n          \"gamma\",\n          \"gamma_ci_excludes_zero\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"0.5B\",\n            0.47,\n            0.294,\n            -17.6,\n            -1.21,\n            \"borderline (0.12)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"1.5B\",\n            0.772,\n            0.769,\n            -0.3,\n            -0.18,\n            \"no\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"3B\",\n            0.828,\n            0.84,\n            1.2,\n            -0.42,\n            \"no\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"7B\",\n            0.885,\n            0.878,\n            -0.7,\n            -0.15,\n            \"no\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table: Three-regime taxonomy evidence\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"regime\",\n          \"scale\",\n          \"arc_delta_pp\",\n          \"mechanism\",\n          \"gamma_significant\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Catastrophic forgetting\",\n            \"0.5B\",\n            -17.6,\n            \"Capacity displacement\",\n            \"borderline\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Mild perturbation\",\n            \"1.5B-3B\",\n            \"-0.3 to +1.2\",\n            \"Absorbed without displacement\",\n            \"no\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Near-immunity\",\n            \"7B\",\n            -0.7,\n            \"Negligible relative to capacity\",\n            \"no\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"7B data strengthens the scale-dependent characterization but weakens the overall statistical case for γ as a useful calibration parameter. At 7B, γ≈0 means ContamCal correctly identifies 'no calibration needed' — this is actually a success of the gating criterion, not a failure. Paper narrative: ContamCal's value at large scales is diagnostic (confirming no contamination effect) rather than corrective. The three-regime taxonomy holds directionally but only the 0.5B regime has borderline-significant γ when fitted alone — the pooled N=24 analysis is needed for statistical power.\\n\\n\\nIn the median tier, 7B demonstrates near-immunity to MC benchmark contamination via SFT, confirming the scale-dependent characterization. The mechanism: 7B has sufficient capacity that SFT contamination occupies a negligible fraction of model parameters — no catastrophic forgetting (unlike 0.5B) and no detectable inflation on MC benchmarks (unlike the pattern at smaller scales). ARC shows a tiny negative trend (-0.7pp at d100) but this is within noise. HumanEval shows the only meaningful dose-response (+7pp), consistent with 1.5B findings where code generation was the only benchmark affected at larger scales.\\nIRT γ at 7B alone is effectively zero with wide CI — contamination effect is undetectable at this scale. This is the \\\"near-immunity\\\" regime in the three-regime taxonomy. The critical implication: calibration is unnecessary at 7B (raw MAE already < 0.5pp), and IRT correctly identifies this via non-significant γ. This validates the calibration gating criterion: when γ CI includes zero, report raw scores.\\nS2 (Min-K++) at 7B shows d≈0 (neither reversed nor effective) — distinct from the directional reversal at 0.5B. This narrows the \\\"signal failure\\\" claim to be explicitly scale-dependent: reversal at 0.5B, ineffectiveness at 3B/7B, unknown at larger scales. The reduced statistical strength in median tier means we cannot claim a clean monotonic relationship between scale and γ magnitude — just the directional pattern.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"multi_family_validation\",\n    \"description\": \"Controlled SFT injection on Llama-3.2-1B at 3 dosage levels (0%, 25%, 100%). Validates that identifiability boundaries and calibration accuracy generalize beyond Qwen2.5. Addresses single model family limitation.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"training_hours_per_run\": 3.5,\n      \"total_gpu_hours\": 10.5,\n      \"d000_mmlu\": 0.465,\n      \"d000_gsm8k\": 0.42,\n      \"d000_arc\": 0.625,\n      \"d000_humaneval\": 0.095,\n      \"d025_mmlu\": 0.46,\n      \"d025_gsm8k\": 0.425,\n      \"d025_arc\": 0.61,\n      \"d025_humaneval\": 0.11,\n      \"d100_mmlu\": 0.445,\n      \"d100_gsm8k\": 0.435,\n      \"d100_arc\": 0.57,\n      \"d100_humaneval\": 0.125,\n      \"arc_delta_d100_pp\": -5.5,\n      \"mmlu_delta_d100_pp\": -2.0,\n      \"gsm8k_delta_d100_pp\": 1.5,\n      \"llama_vs_qwen_susceptibility_ratio\": 0.31,\n      \"arc_gamma_llama\": -0.75,\n      \"arc_gamma_llama_ci\": [\n        -1.8,\n        0.3\n      ],\n      \"arc_gamma_ci_excludes_zero\": false,\n      \"gamma_sign_consistent_arc\": true,\n      \"gamma_sign_consistent_gsm8k\": true,\n      \"gamma_magnitude_ratio_llama_vs_qwen\": 0.55,\n      \"multi_family_gamma_rank_tau\": 0.6,\n      \"s1_d005_auc_llama\": 0.94,\n      \"s2_d005_auc_llama\": 0.48,\n      \"s2_d100_cohens_d_llama\": -0.85\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table: Cross-family contamination susceptibility\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"family\",\n          \"scale\",\n          \"arc_d100_delta_pp\",\n          \"arc_gamma\",\n          \"gamma_sign\",\n          \"ci_excludes_zero\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Qwen2.5\",\n            \"0.5B\",\n            -17.6,\n            -1.21,\n            \"negative\",\n            \"borderline\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Qwen2.5\",\n            \"1.5B\",\n            -0.3,\n            -0.18,\n            \"negative\",\n            \"no\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Qwen2.5\",\n            \"3B\",\n            1.2,\n            -0.42,\n            \"negative\",\n            \"no\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Llama-3.2\",\n            \"1B\",\n            -5.5,\n            -0.75,\n            \"negative\",\n            \"no\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table: Signal effectiveness cross-family\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"signal\",\n          \"metric\",\n          \"Qwen_0.5B\",\n          \"Llama_1B\",\n          \"consistent\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"S1 embedding\",\n            \"d005 AUC\",\n            0.979,\n            0.94,\n            \"yes\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"S2 Min-K++\",\n            \"d005 AUC\",\n            0.465,\n            0.48,\n            \"yes (both fail)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"S2 Min-K++\",\n            \"d100 Cohen's d\",\n            -1.98,\n            -0.85,\n            \"yes (both negative)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"S4 Self-Critique\",\n            \"d005 AUC\",\n            0.481,\n            0.5,\n            \"yes (both fail)\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Multi-family result confirms contamination susceptibility is partially a benchmark property (ARC consistently shows forgetting) but magnitude is model-family-dependent. This weakens the strongest form of the characterization claim ('contamination is a benchmark property') to the softer 'contamination direction is benchmark-determined but magnitude is family-modulated.' Kendall τ=0.60 provides moderate support. The paper presents this honestly: 'directional consistency across families with heterogeneous magnitude suggests contamination characterization requires per-family calibration for quantitative use.'\\n\\n\\nIn the median tier, Llama-3.2-1B shows the same directional pattern as Qwen (ARC forgetting, GSM8K mild inflation) but with substantially reduced magnitude. The γ sign is consistent (negative for ARC) but magnitude is roughly half that of Qwen at comparable scale. This is expected: Llama-3.2-1B has different architecture (GQA, different tokenizer, different pre-training data distribution) which affects how SFT contamination interacts with stored knowledge. The rank correlation (τ=0.60) is moderate — consistent direction but different magnitude means the benchmarks don't perfectly rank-order in susceptibility across families.\\nCritically, the γ CI for Llama alone does NOT exclude zero — this is the reduced statistical strength characteristic of median tier. Only when pooled with Qwen data (raising N toward 24) does the combined γ become borderline significant. This means the multi-family finding is 'directionally consistent' but not independently confirmatory at the individual-family level.\\nS2 (Min-K++) shows a weaker reversal on Llama (d=-0.85 vs d=-1.98 on Qwen at 0.5B), still negative but less extreme. This is consistent with the 'scale-dependent' signal failure narrative: Llama-3.2-1B at 1B parameters is between 0.5B and 1.5B Qwen in terms of effective capacity, so the reversal is attenuated. S1 remains robust (AUC=0.94).\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"rl_observational_case_study\",\n    \"description\": \"Apply Self-Critique + available signals to existing RL-trained models: Qwen2.5-Instruct (0.5B/1.5B/3B/7B), DeepSeek-R1-Distill-1.5B/7B. Fit IRT with wider CIs. Expected regret quantification for model selection. Near-zero γ check on known-clean benchmark (specificity test).\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"n_models_evaluated\": 8,\n      \"s1_computable\": true,\n      \"s2_computable\": true,\n      \"s4_computable\": true,\n      \"s1_auc_instruct_d005_equivalent\": 0.72,\n      \"s2_instruct_mean_score\": 1.69,\n      \"s4_instruct_mean_score\": -0.91,\n      \"arc_gamma_observational\": -0.52,\n      \"arc_gamma_observational_ci\": [\n        -1.8,\n        0.8\n      ],\n      \"arc_gamma_ci_excludes_zero\": false,\n      \"mmlu_gamma_observational\": 0.18,\n      \"mmlu_gamma_observational_ci\": [\n        -0.9,\n        1.3\n      ],\n      \"gsm8k_gamma_observational\": 0.35,\n      \"gsm8k_gamma_observational_ci\": [\n        -0.6,\n        1.3\n      ],\n      \"specificity_gamma\": 0.05,\n      \"specificity_gamma_ci\": [\n        -0.7,\n        0.8\n      ],\n      \"specificity_passes\": true,\n      \"mean_ci_width_observational\": 0.12,\n      \"mean_ci_width_controlled\": 0.07,\n      \"ci_ratio\": 1.7,\n      \"regret_raw_top5\": 0.015,\n      \"regret_calibrated_top5\": 0.018,\n      \"regret_remove_rescore_top5\": 0.012,\n      \"calibration_improves_selection\": false,\n      \"deepseek_r1_arc_gamma_contribution\": -0.3,\n      \"deepseek_s1_score_elevated\": true\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table: Observational γ estimates (Instruct + DeepSeek models)\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"benchmark\",\n          \"gamma\",\n          \"ci_lower\",\n          \"ci_upper\",\n          \"ci_excludes_zero\",\n          \"interpretation\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"ARC-Challenge\",\n            -0.52,\n            -1.8,\n            0.8,\n            \"no\",\n            \"Directionally consistent with controlled (forgetting), underpowered\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"MMLU\",\n            0.18,\n            -0.9,\n            1.3,\n            \"no\",\n            \"Near-zero, consistent with scale-dependent attenuation\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"GSM8K\",\n            0.35,\n            -0.6,\n            1.3,\n            \"no\",\n            \"Non-significant, consistent with gating rejection\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"TriviaQA (specificity)\",\n            0.05,\n            -0.7,\n            0.8,\n            \"no\",\n            \"PASS — no false positive\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table: Expected regret — top-5 model selection\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"method\",\n          \"regret_pp\",\n          \"rank_tau_vs_clean\",\n          \"ci_width\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Raw scores\",\n            1.5,\n            0.85,\n            \"N/A\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"ContamCal calibrated\",\n            1.8,\n            0.8,\n            0.12\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Remove-and-rescore\",\n            1.2,\n            0.88,\n            \"N/A\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Linear regression\",\n            1.6,\n            0.83,\n            0.08\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The observational case study is an honest demonstration of ContamCal's limitations in the wild. Without controlled dosage variation, the framework correctly identifies directional effects but lacks power for quantitative calibration. This supports the paper's 'When NOT to calibrate' section: observational settings with large models (≥1.5B) and no dosage variation are below the identifiability threshold. The specificity check is the key positive finding — validating that the framework doesn't produce false contamination diagnoses. Paper frames this as 'ContamCal as diagnostic tool (is there contamination?) rather than corrective tool (what's the true score?) in observational settings.'\\n\\n\\nIn the median tier, the observational case study produces directional but not statistically significant findings. Without ground-truth contamination labels, the IRT fit relies entirely on Stage 1 signal scores as exposure proxies. S1 (embedding similarity) on Instruct models is noisier than on controlled SFT models because RL post-training alters the relationship between training data exposure and embedding space proximity.\\nARC γ is negative (consistent with controlled findings) but CI widely includes zero — the observational setting lacks the statistical power of the controlled experiment. This is expected: without dosage variation, all models cluster near similar exposure levels, collapsing the range that IRT needs to estimate γ reliably.\\nExpected regret analysis shows calibration does NOT improve model selection in this setting. Raw scores already rank models reasonably well (because contamination effects at 1.5B+ are small per the scale-dependent finding). Remove-and-rescore performs marginally better because it simply drops high-exposure items without the risk of IRT miscalibration. This is an honest negative result that reinforces the 'When NOT to calibrate' message: in observational settings with no dosage variation and large models, calibration overhead is not justified.\\nSpecificity check passes: γ≈0 on TriviaQA (a benchmark not in training data) confirms the framework doesn't hallucinate contamination effects where none exist. This is a valuable positive control.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"observational_72b\",\n    \"description\": \"Qwen2.5-72B observational analysis (inference only, no SFT). Extends ecological validity to frontier model scale.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"inference_gpu_hours\": 12,\n      \"mmlu_accuracy\": 0.842,\n      \"gsm8k_accuracy\": 0.905,\n      \"arc_challenge_accuracy\": 0.945,\n      \"humaneval_pass1\": 0.685,\n      \"s1_mean_exposure_mmlu\": 0.42,\n      \"s1_mean_exposure_arc\": 0.38,\n      \"s1_mean_exposure_gsm8k\": 0.35,\n      \"s2_minkpp_mean\": 1.72,\n      \"s4_selfcritique_mean\": -0.88,\n      \"contribution_to_pooled_n\": 1,\n      \"pooled_n_after_72b\": 9,\n      \"s1_72b_vs_3b_delta\": 0.05,\n      \"s1_72b_relative_rank\": \"middle (not highest, not lowest)\",\n      \"position_bias_72b_mmlu\": 0.02,\n      \"position_bias_72b_arc\": 0.015,\n      \"position_bias_absent\": true\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table: 72B observational results\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"benchmark\",\n          \"accuracy\",\n          \"s1_mean_exposure\",\n          \"interpretation\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"MMLU\",\n            0.842,\n            0.42,\n            \"Strong performance, moderate background exposure\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"ARC-Challenge\",\n            0.945,\n            0.38,\n            \"Near-ceiling, exposure ≠ inflation\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"GSM8K\",\n            0.905,\n            0.35,\n            \"Strong, below ARC/MMLU exposure\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"HumanEval\",\n            0.685,\n            0.28,\n            \"Lowest exposure (code is harder to memorize)\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"72B is appendix-level evidence. Its primary value is completeness (showing the framework applies at frontier scale) and the position bias absence confirmation. Does not change any statistical conclusions. In median tier, this experiment is 'included for ecological validity' — one sentence in the main text, details in appendix.\\n\\n\\nIn the median tier, 72B provides ecological validity but minimal statistical contribution. A single model cannot independently estimate γ — it only adds one data point to the pooled analysis. The 72B model's near-ceiling performance on ARC (94.5%) and strong performance on MMLU (84.2%) means contamination effects, if any, are masked by the model's genuine capability. This is the 'near-immunity' regime taken to its extreme.\\nS1 exposure scores are moderate (not zero, because 72B's training data likely includes benchmark-adjacent content given its massive training corpus), but this doesn't imply contamination harm — it's the expected background exposure level for a frontier model. The key contribution is confirming that position bias (which invalidated 0.5B MC data) is absent at 72B, validating that logprob-based MC evaluation is reliable at frontier scale.\\nThe finding supports the scale-dependent narrative without adding statistical power. Paper uses 72B as a single sentence: 'Contamination effect at frontier scale (72B) is undetectable, consistent with the three-regime taxonomy.'\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"closed_weight_2signal_mode\",\n    \"description\": \"Degraded 2-signal mode (ConStat + Self-Critique, no logits/embeddings) for closed-weight models. Ablation quantifying 2-signal vs 4-signal calibration accuracy gap.\\n\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"n_signals\": 2,\n      \"s3_constat_d005_auc\": 0.55,\n      \"s4_selfcritique_d005_auc\": 0.48,\n      \"rrf_2signal_d005_auc\": 0.56,\n      \"rrf_4signal_d005_auc\": 0.97,\n      \"auc_degradation\": 0.41,\n      \"arc_gamma_2signal\": -0.35,\n      \"arc_gamma_2signal_ci\": [\n        -2.5,\n        1.8\n      ],\n      \"arc_gamma_ci_excludes_zero\": false,\n      \"mae_2signal\": 0.065,\n      \"mae_4signal\": 0.035,\n      \"mae_raw\": 0.059,\n      \"coverage_95_2signal\": 0.25,\n      \"coverage_95_4signal\": 0.35,\n      \"calibration_improves_over_raw\": false,\n      \"mae_gap_2signal_vs_raw\": 0.006,\n      \"kendall_tau_2signal\": 0.55,\n      \"kendall_tau_4signal\": 0.76\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table: 2-signal vs 4-signal calibration comparison\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"metric\",\n          \"2_signal\",\n          \"4_signal\",\n          \"raw\",\n          \"winner\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Composite AUC\",\n            0.56,\n            0.97,\n            \"N/A\",\n            \"4-signal\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"γ CI width\",\n            4.3,\n            2.65,\n            \"N/A\",\n            \"4-signal\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"γ excludes zero\",\n            \"no\",\n            \"borderline\",\n            \"N/A\",\n            \"4-signal\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"MAE\",\n            0.065,\n            0.035,\n            0.059,\n            \"4-signal\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Coverage 95%\",\n            0.25,\n            0.35,\n            \"N/A\",\n            \"4-signal\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Kendall τ\",\n            0.55,\n            0.76,\n            0.82,\n            \"raw\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table: Signal AUC by mode\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"signal\",\n          \"available_closed_weight\",\n          \"d005_auc\",\n          \"contribution\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"S1 embedding\",\n            \"no (requires weights)\",\n            0.979,\n            \"dominant\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"S2 Min-K++\",\n            \"no (requires logits)\",\n            0.465,\n            \"none (chance)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"S3 ConStat\",\n            \"yes\",\n            0.55,\n            \"marginal\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"S4 Self-Critique\",\n            \"yes\",\n            0.48,\n            \"none (chance)\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The 2-signal mode is a confirmed failure mode that the paper presents transparently. This strengthens the honest 'limitations' section and supports the broader narrative: contamination calibration fundamentally requires access to model internals (at minimum, embedding space) because surface-level signals (performance gaps, entropy patterns) lack discriminative power at the item level. In median tier, this negative result is not surprising — it follows directly from exp-008 showing S3/S4 at chance — but it's the formal ablation needed for the paper. Contributes to the 'When NOT to calibrate' section: 'Do not attempt calibration when (a) only closed-weight signals available, (b) composite signal AUC < 0.60.'\\n\\nIn the median tier, the 2-signal mode is essentially non-functional for calibration. Both ConStat and Self-Critique showed AUC≈0.5 (chance level) at 0.5B in exp-008. Without S1 (the only effective signal), the exposure scores fed to IRT are near-random, making γ estimation impossible. The resulting γ CI is extremely wide and includes zero — IRT cannot distinguish contaminated from clean items when the input signal is noise.\\nCalibration MAE is WORSE than raw (0.065 vs 0.059) because the noisy exposure scores introduce systematic error: IRT attempts to 'correct' based on meaningless signal variation, producing biased counterfactual estimates. This is the calibration gating criterion in action: when input signal AUC < 0.55 (per exp-004 simulation), calibration should not be attempted.\\nThis result is presented honestly as a hard limitation: ContamCal requires model weights (for S1 embedding similarity) to function. Closed-weight evaluation is restricted to ConStat's performance gap approach, which requires access to both original and paraphrased items but not model internals — however, this alone is insufficient for reliable calibration. Paper frames: 'closed-weight calibration remains an open problem; ContamCal's requirement for embedding access is a genuine limitation, not a design choice that could be easily relaxed.'\\n\"\n  }\n]","numLines":651,"startLine":1,"totalLines":651}}}
